      "id": 1,
      "text": "Royal Lakes began as a 320‑acre resort plan bought by a Chicago developer in 1956.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Lakes%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Three man‑made lakes—Meshach, Shad, and Shadrach—were built to give residents a beach‑style feel.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Lakes%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The first families moved in 1957, but the community struggled without roads, water, or sewage until the early 1970s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        12,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Lakes%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A vote in October 1972 saw 89 of 98 residents favor incorporation, turning the area into an official village in 1973.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        15,
        16,
        17,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Lakes%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Meshach Lake, the deepest of the trio, averages 9 to 12 feet in depth and sits in the village’s east‑central part.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Royal_Lakes%2C_Illinois",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Shad Lake, the shallowest, covers about 1.5 acres and sits on Coop Branch, a tributary of Macoupin Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
